<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="connect-a-cli-client"
      updated="August 21, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"stdio-bridge", "Set up a stdio client"},
        {"direct-http", "Direct HTTP — no bridge"},
        {"verify", "Verify the connection"},
        {"sign-in-grants", "What the sign-in grants"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="connect-a-cli-client">
        <:dek>
          Connect a local AI client to your action catalog through direct HTTPS or the
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar-mcp</code>
          stdio bridge.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Using Claude.ai or ChatGPT instead? Those connect over OAuth with no key or bridge to
        manage — see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/connect-an-llm"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Connect a cloud LLM</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          An account with at least one runner online. Otherwise the agent signs in fine and finds
          an empty catalog with nothing to call. New here? Run the
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
            quickstart
          </.link>
          first, then come back to wire in your client.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.agent_skill_cta
        class="mt-6"
        skill="connect-llm"
        prompt="Connect this machine's coding agent to my emisar account over MCP — install the emisar-mcp bridge, register the client, and prove a run end to end. First ask me which client I use. Use the connect-llm skill: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AndrewDryga/emisar/main/skills/connect-llm/SKILL.md"
      >
        The <code class="text-zinc-300">connect-llm</code>
        skill walks your agent through this whole page.
      </.agent_skill_cta>

      <%!-- ============================================================ --%>
      <%!-- Stdio bridge                                                  --%>
      <%!-- ============================================================ --%>
      <section>
        <.docs_h2 id="stdio-bridge">Set up a stdio client</.docs_h2>

        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-zinc-400">
          Most local clients speak stdio. The
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar-mcp</code>
          bridge is a small binary that sits in between the agent and emisar control plane. Your
          client starts it, and it passes each call to your account. Install it once per machine —
          every client uses the same one.
        </p>

        <.docs_h3 id="install-bridge">Install</.docs_h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-base text-zinc-300">
          On macOS or Linux, this command resolves the latest
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">mcp-v*</code>
          release, verifies its checksum, and installs
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar-mcp</code>
          into <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/usr/local/bin</code>:
        </p>
        <.docs_code
          phx-no-format
          label="shell"
          copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash"
        ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL <span class="text-brand-300">https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh</span> | sudo bash</.docs_code>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base text-zinc-300">
          On 64-bit Windows, open PowerShell and run:
        </p>
        <.docs_code
          phx-no-format
          label="PowerShell"
          copy_text="irm https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.ps1 | iex"
        ><span class="text-zinc-500">PS&gt;</span> irm <span class="text-brand-300">https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.ps1</span> | iex</.docs_code>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 text-zinc-400">
          Both installers find supported clients. You choose which clients to connect. Browser
          approval creates one key for direct <code>emisar-mcp</code>
          commands and a separate key for each selected client. Direct commands then work without
          shell-profile exports. The installer preserves comments and other MCP servers in each
          client config. See <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/mcp-reference#command-line"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Use MCP tools from the shell</.link>.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 text-zinc-400">
          The list of tools comes from emisar, not from the bridge. An installed bridge keeps
          seeing current tools on its own. Update it when emisar raises the lowest version it
          supports.
        </p>
        <.docs_verify_download
          tarball="emisar-mcp-<version>-linux-amd64.tar.gz"
          checksums="SHA256SUMS-MCP"
        />

        <details class="group mt-8 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-zinc-900 bg-black/40">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-4 text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100 transition-colors hover:bg-zinc-900/30 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
            <span class="flex items-center gap-2">
              <.icon name="hero-wrench-screwdriver" class="h-4 w-4 text-zinc-500" />
              Set up a client by hand
            </span>
            <.icon
              name="hero-chevron-down"
              class="h-5 w-5 shrink-0 text-zinc-500 transition duration-200 group-hover:text-zinc-300 group-open:rotate-180 group-open:text-brand-400"
            />
          </summary>
          <div class="border-t border-zinc-900 px-5 pb-5 pt-4 space-y-8">
            <p class="text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
              Every snippet here needs an agent key. Open <a
                href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
                class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
              >Connect an agent</a>, pick your client, and create its key. The console prefills the whole
              snippet for you, and the key is shown once.
            </p>

            <%!-- Claude Desktop --%>
            <section>
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">Claude Desktop</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-base text-zinc-400">
                Add to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json</code>:
              </p>
              <.docs_code phx-no-format label="json" copy_id="mcp-config-claude-desktop">&#123;
  "mcpServers": &#123;
    "emisar": &#123;
      "command": "/usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp",
      "env": &#123;
        "EMISAR_URL": "https://emisar.dev",
        "EMISAR_API_KEY": "emk-...",
        "EMISAR_CLIENT": "claude-desktop"
      &#125;
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</.docs_code>
              <p class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
                Restart Claude Desktop. Tools appear under the tool icon
                as "emisar".
              </p>
            </section>

            <%!-- Cursor --%>
            <section>
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">Cursor</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-base text-zinc-400">
                Same bridge. Cursor reads <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">~/.cursor/mcp.json</code>:
              </p>
              <.docs_code phx-no-format label="json" copy_id="mcp-config-cursor">&#123;
  "mcpServers": &#123;
    "emisar": &#123;
      "command": "/usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp",
      "env": &#123;
        "EMISAR_URL": "https://emisar.dev",
        "EMISAR_API_KEY": "emk-...",
        "EMISAR_CLIENT": "cursor"
      &#125;
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</.docs_code>
              <p class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
                Recent Cursor builds also speak remote MCP. Point them at
                <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/api/mcp/rpc</code>
                directly (see <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/connect-an-llm"}
                  class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
                >Connect a cloud LLM</.link>) to skip the bridge.
              </p>
            </section>

            <%!-- VS Code --%>
            <section id="vscode">
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">VS Code</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
                Run
                <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-200">MCP: Open User Configuration</strong>
                from the Command Palette and add this server. VS Code asks for the key when it
                first starts the bridge, so the secret does not live in a settings file that may
                sync to another machine.
              </p>
              <.docs_code phx-no-format label="jsonc" copy_id="mcp-config-vscode">&#123;
  "inputs": [
    &#123;
      "type": "promptString",
      "id": "emisar-api-key",
      "description": "Emisar API key",
      "password": true
    &#125;
  ],
  "servers": &#123;
    "emisar": &#123;
      "type": "stdio",
      "command": "emisar-mcp",
      "args": [],
      "env": &#123;
        "EMISAR_URL": "https://emisar.dev",
        "EMISAR_API_KEY": "$&#123;input:emisar-api-key&#125;",
        "EMISAR_CLIENT": "vscode"
      &#125;
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</.docs_code>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-6 text-zinc-400">
                The installer configures the stable default VS Code profile automatically and
                stores its key in an owner-only environment file outside <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">mcp.json</code>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <%!-- Zed --%>
            <section>
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">Zed</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-base text-zinc-400">
                By hand, Zed reads
                <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">
                  ~/.config/zed/settings.json
                </code>
                and nests MCP servers under <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">context_servers</code>, not <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">mcpServers</code>:
              </p>
              <.docs_code phx-no-format label="json" copy_id="mcp-config-zed">&#123;
  "context_servers": &#123;
    "emisar": &#123;
      "source": "custom",
      "command": "/usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp",
      "args": [],
      "env": &#123;
        "EMISAR_URL": "https://emisar.dev",
        "EMISAR_API_KEY": "emk-...",
        "EMISAR_CLIENT": "zed"
      &#125;
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</.docs_code>
            </section>

            <%!-- Claude Code (CLI) --%>
            <section>
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">Claude Code</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-base text-zinc-400">
                One-line register, user scope (every project):
              </p>
              <.docs_code
                phx-no-format
                label="terminal"
                copy_text="claude mcp add emisar --scope user -e EMISAR_URL=https://emisar.dev -e EMISAR_API_KEY=emk-... -e EMISAR_CLIENT=claude-code -- /usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp"
              ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> claude mcp add emisar --scope user \
    -e EMISAR_URL=https://emisar.dev \
    -e EMISAR_API_KEY=emk-... \
    -e EMISAR_CLIENT=claude-code \
    -- /usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp</.docs_code>
              <p class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
                Verify with <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">claude mcp list</code>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <%!-- Gemini CLI --%>
            <section>
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">Gemini CLI</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-base text-zinc-400">
                Add to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">~/.gemini/settings.json</code>:
              </p>
              <.docs_code phx-no-format label="json" copy_id="mcp-config-gemini">&#123;
  "mcpServers": &#123;
    "emisar": &#123;
      "command": "/usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp",
      "env": &#123;
        "EMISAR_URL": "https://emisar.dev",
        "EMISAR_API_KEY": "emk-...",
        "EMISAR_CLIENT": "gemini"
      &#125;
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</.docs_code>
            </section>

            <%!-- ChatGPT Codex CLI --%>
            <section>
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">ChatGPT Codex CLI</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-base text-zinc-400">
                Add to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">~/.codex/config.toml</code>:
              </p>
              <.docs_code phx-no-format label="toml" copy_id="mcp-config-codex">[mcp_servers.emisar]
command = "/usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp"
env = &#123; EMISAR_URL = "https://emisar.dev", EMISAR_API_KEY = "emk-...", EMISAR_CLIENT = "codex" &#125;</.docs_code>
              <p class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
                To let emisar calls run without Codex prompts, add
                <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">
                  default_tools_approval_mode = "approve"
                </code>
                below
                <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">[mcp_servers.emisar]</code>
                in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">~/.codex/config.toml</code>.
                This trusts only the emisar MCP server. Emisar still applies its own policies and approvals.
              </p>
            </section>

            <%!-- Grok CLI --%>
            <section>
              <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100">Grok CLI</h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-base text-zinc-400">
                Register the bridge in your user config:
              </p>
              <.docs_code
                phx-no-format
                label="terminal"
                copy_text="grok mcp add emisar -e EMISAR_URL=https://emisar.dev -e EMISAR_API_KEY=emk-... -e EMISAR_CLIENT=grok -- /usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp"
              ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> grok mcp add emisar \
    -e EMISAR_URL=https://emisar.dev \
    -e EMISAR_API_KEY=emk-... \
    -e EMISAR_CLIENT=grok \
    -- /usr/local/bin/emisar-mcp</.docs_code>
              <p class="mt-3 text-base text-zinc-400">
                To stop Grok's per-tool prompts for emisar only, add
                <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">"MCPTool(emisar__*)"</code>
                to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">[permission].allow</code>
                in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">~/.grok/config.toml</code>.
              </p>
            </section>
          </div>
        </details>

        <.docs_callout kind={:note}>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-zinc-400">Targeting a signed-dispatch runner?</strong>
            If a runner enforces <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/signed-dispatch"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >signed dispatch</.link>, the bridge needs
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">EMISAR_SIGNING_KEY</code>
            and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">EMISAR_SIGNING_CERT</code>
            in its environment. Put them wherever your client passes environment to the bridge: the
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">env</code>
            block in its config file, or another
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">-e</code>
            flag if you registered it with
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">claude mcp add</code>
            or <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">grok mcp add</code>.
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar signing init</code>
            prints both values. The bridge signs each action dispatch. Keep both values secret and
            off the portal.
          </p>
        </.docs_callout>
      </section>

      <section>
        <.docs_h2 id="direct-http">Direct HTTP — no bridge</.docs_h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-base text-zinc-400">
          Any MCP client that supports Streamable HTTP and a custom authorization header can
          connect straight to emisar. There is no bridge binary to install.
        </p>

        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base text-zinc-300">
          <li>
            Open <a
              href={~p"/app/agents/connect"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Connect an agent</a>, select <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-200">Custom</strong>,
            create a dedicated key, and copy it before leaving the page. The key is shown once.
          </li>
          <li>
            Add an MCP server to your client, choose the
            <strong class="font-medium text-zinc-200">Streamable HTTP</strong>
            transport, and give it these two values:
          </li>
        </ol>
        <.docs_value label="Server URL" copy_text="https://emisar.dev/api/mcp/rpc">
          https://emisar.dev/api/mcp/rpc
        </.docs_value>
        <.docs_value
          label="Authorization header"
          copy_text="Authorization: Bearer emk-..."
        >
          Authorization: Bearer emk-...
        </.docs_value>
        <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          Replace <code>emk-...</code> with the key you copied. Store it in the client's secret or
          user-level configuration. Do not commit it in a project configuration file.
        </p>

        <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="What direct HTTP leaves out">
          <p>
            A direct HTTP client cannot sign dispatches and does not rotate its agent key
            automatically. Use the stdio bridge when a runner requires <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/signed-dispatch"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >signed dispatch</.link>. Rotate or revoke a direct client's key from <a
              href={~p"/app/agents"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >LLM agents</a>.
          </p>
        </.docs_callout>
      </section>

      <.docs_h2 id="verify">Verify the connection</.docs_h2>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base text-zinc-300">
        <li>Restart or reconnect the client after its config changes.</li>
        <li>Confirm that the client lists the emisar MCP tools.</li>
        <li>
          Ask it to run <code>linux.uptime</code> on any one of the connected runners.
        </li>
        <li>Confirm that the client returns the action output.</li>
        <li>
          Open <a
            href={~p"/app/audit"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Audit</a>. Confirm that the event names the client, runner, action, and reason.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="sign-in-grants">What the sign-in grants</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Whether the installer minted it after your browser approval or you pasted one by hand, the
        key is bound to <em>your</em>
        membership. The agent acts as you. Every call is attributed to you and the agent you are using. It reaches only the
        runners and actions your own access already allows (<.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/keys#no-scope"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >how a key inherits its operator's scope</.link>). Revoke it any time from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/agents"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >LLM agents</a>
        in the console.
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/keys"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          Agent keys
        </.link>
        covers rotation.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_cta
    headline="Point your agent at real infrastructure — safely."
    subcopy="Set up a runner first, then connect Claude Code, Cursor, or any MCP client to your account's endpoint and let policy decide what runs."
    secondary_label="Read the quickstart"
    secondary_path={~p"/docs/quickstart"}
  />

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
